  "summary": "Streets painted overnight in sawdust cathedrals — then walked to dust by morning.",
  "description": "In the weeks before Easter, families and brotherhoods in Antigua Guatemala transform cobbled streets into alfombras — carpets of dyed sawdust, pine needles, flowers and fruit laid in baroque designs:Processional path maps, christs' monograms, Mayan-bird motifs and cathedral facades drawn in stenciled color gradients. Each carpet is built in a night vigil and then destroyed by the very procession it guides, the anda floats and incense thuribles grinding it to powder — an art of maximum effort and zero permanence that draws hundreds of thousands of visitors and embodies Guatemalan devotional creativity at street scale.",
  "culturalContext": "Guatemala's alfombras are devotion measured in perishability: families and cofradías build street carpets in overnight vigils that processions destroy by morning, an art of maximum effort and zero permanence. The practice blends Maya color cosmology with Spanish procession culture and draws the world to Antigua each Semana Santa.",
